# ==============================================================================
# AquariusOS — the logo in the top-left corner of the screen
# ==============================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE CHANGES
#   The little logo button at the far left of GNOME's top bar. Clicking it opens
#   a menu (App Grid, Files, Steam, Lutris, …). It is not part of GNOME: it comes
#   from an extension called Logo Menu, which Bazzite installs and switches on.
#
#   On the first bench boot of the GNOME line (2026-08-31) that button was still
#   showing Bazzite's purple logo. This file is what makes it ours.
#
# HOW A FILE IN THIS FOLDER REACHES THE DESKTOP, IN PLAIN ENGLISH
#   GNOME stores settings in a database called dconf. Files in
#   /etc/dconf/db/distro.d/ are plain text, and `dconf update` bakes the whole
#   folder into one database that every account on the machine reads underneath
#   its own choices. Drop a file in here and run nothing and NOTHING happens,
#   with no error anywhere — the same trap as the .gschema.override files, and
#   the reason build_files/gnome-desktop.sh runs `dconf update` for us.
#
#   ⚠️ THIS IS A DIFFERENT MECHANISM FROM OUR OTHER GNOME DEFAULTS, and the
#   difference is not a style choice. Everything else we set lives in
#   /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas/zz1-aquarius-*.gschema.override, which changes
#   what a setting's FACTORY VALUE is. That only works for settings that have a
#   published description (a "schema") installed system-wide. Logo Menu keeps
#   its schema inside the extension folder instead, so there is nothing in
#   /usr/share/glib-2.0/schemas for an override file to attach to — and a
#   .gschema.override naming a setting that does not exist there is a build
#   failure under --strict. dconf is the mechanism that works for this one, and
#   it is the mechanism Bazzite itself uses for the same extension.
#
# WHY THE NAME STARTS WITH "zz1-"
#   When two files in this folder set the same thing, THE ONE THAT SORTS LAST
#   WINS. That is not folklore — it is written into dconf itself, in
#   bin/dconf.c, as the comment "FILES-PRECEDENCE: When a path is found in
#   multiple files, value from the file lexicographically latest takes
#   precedence."
#
#   Bazzite's files here are called 00-bazzite-…, 01-bazzite-… and
#   10-bazzite-deck-…-logomenu. "zz1-aquarius-logomenu" sorts after all of them,
#   so our answer is the one that survives. It is the same prefix, for the same
#   reason, as our .gschema.override files. build_files/gnome-desktop.sh checks
#   that this is still true rather than trusting the convention, and so does CI.
#
# THE SETTING ITSELF
#   Logo Menu decides which picture to show in this order (its display_module.js):
#     1. use-custom-icon is on AND custom-icon-path is not empty → use that file
#     2. otherwise, one of its own built-in distro logos, picked by number
#   So turning the first one on is enough. We do not have to know, or keep in
#   step with, which number Bazzite happens to be.
#
#   ⚠️ The path must point at a file that really exists. If it does not, the
#   extension quietly falls back to a generic grey icon and says nothing.
#   gnome-desktop.sh checks the file is there and fails the build if it is not.
#
#   The file named below is the SAME logo icon the rest of the OS uses — the one
#   os-release's LOGO field names. There is deliberately only one logo icon on
#   the machine; see branding/README.md.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO
#   It is not a lock. Anyone can open the extension's own settings and pick a
#   different icon, and their choice wins from then on. Nothing here overwrites
#   a choice somebody has made.
# ==============================================================================

[org/gnome/shell/extensions/Logo-menu]
use-custom-icon=true
custom-icon-path='/usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/aquarius-logo.svg'
